 "result_text": "I changed `Light.set_light` in `src/uiprotect/data/devices.py` so it can control a floodlight that Protect turned on or off. I didn't run or add any tests.\n\n**Cause (my reading, not reproduced):** `set_light` only sends a request when its local diff is non-empty. If Protect turns the light on (for example on motion), `is_led_force_on` stays `False`. Calling `set_light(False)` then produces no change, so nothing is sent and the light stays on.\n\n**Fix:** when the force-on flag already equals the requested value but `is_light_on` doesn't, `set_light` now sends the PATCH directly through `update_device`. The body is `lightOnSettings.isLedForceOn`, plus `lightDeviceSettings.ledLevel` if a level was passed. In every other case it behaves as before.\n\nThe direct path returns without updating the local `light_on_settings` object. It relies on Protect's websocket update to refresh state.",
